  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nTranquil weather will prevail through much of the 7-day forecast \nperiod as a zonal split-flow jet stream pattern evolves into a more \namplified jet stream pattern putting western Kansas in a dry \nnorthwest or north-northwest wind pattern aloft. In the short term \ntonight and tomorrow, a weak MSLP gradient will continue to be the \ntheme going into tomorrow due to the aforementioned split jet stream \nto our north and south. Residual near-surface moisture from recent \nwet period(s) will keep dewpoints just high enough to support \nanother morning of ground fog development early Sunday morning. The \nlatest HRRR and REFS ensembles suggest fog development in the Garden \nCity area and also the Red Hills region, but in the official \nforecast, we will connect these two modeled signals with a more \nuniform \"patchy fog\" Wx grid from Scott City to Dodge City to \nGreensburg to Medicine Lodge and points south. Confidence in \nwidespread dense fog is too low, however, to consider a dense fog \nheadline, although evening and overnight shifts will need to monitor \nHRRR and observation/satellite trends closely.\n\nThe split jet pattern over western Kansas will continue to have \nenough of a downslope component, and along with direct insolation, \ntemperatures will continue to track toward the 75th percentile NBM \nwith afternoon temperatures well into the 60s to lower 70s Sunday \ninto at least Tuesday. The main (northern) branch of the jet stream \nwill amplify quite considerably mid-week with the amplification \nshowing strong ridging across the western CONUS and a mean trough in \nthe eastern CONUS. This is a dry pattern for western Kansas this \ntime of year, so the official forecast will remain dry through the \nend of this forecast period (next weekend).",
